  "notes": "Queried directly against assets/data/catalog/baltic_patrol.db (platform, platform_mount, platform_magazine tables) for domain/class/nationality and real magazine-line/mount counts -- no invented catalog rows. Sensor category labels (radar/fire-control, passive-sonar, sonar-dipping, sonar-buoy) follow the same domain+platformClass heuristic tier-3/roster.json consistently used, since the catalog's sensor table stores only opaque per-import numeric IDs (cmo-sensor-NNNN) with no descriptive category column to read back -- there is no real per-platform sensor-type lookup in this schema. 14 of 29 platforms are drawn from corpus/coverage-map.json's underusedPlatformHint list (a-19-gotland-2002, mig-31-foxhound, pla-971-akula-i-improved-shchuka-b, s-181-u-31-type-212a, s-185-u-35-type-212a, eurofighter-typhoon, nh90-tth-mrh90-taipan-2007, pl-677-lada-b-585-sankt-peterburg-2011, pla-885-severodvinsk-yasen, s-120-papanikolis-type-214hn, ssn-774-virginia-blk-i-ii, su-24m-fencer-d-1992, su-30sm-flanker-g-2019, plus pl-877-kilo-paltus-1992 which recurs from tier-3), directly serving the platform-swap-underused recipe. plarb-955a-borei-ii-borey-a (SSBN) is included deliberately as an ASW high-value target, not a combatant with an offensive loadout -- a judgement call, flagged for scenario authors to treat it as a screened/protected or hunted asset rather than a shooter. Remaining recurring surface/AAW-fighter/ASW-helo/MPA platforms are carried over from tier-3's roster.json to preserve continuity and give both sides real ASW screening + AAW intercept capability."
